# Deliberately no blanket "*.pem" here -- this repo's sync workflow
# (sync-public-vault.yml, chameleon-key-vault) legitimately needs to publish
# public-only .pem fixtures (test/fixtures/freetsa-root-ca.pem,
# freetsa-tsa-cert.pem -- a CA cert and a TSA signing cert, no private key
# material). A blanket pattern here silently dropped them from `git add -A`
# on every sync run for a month, even though rsync copied them in every
# time and the sync itself reported success -- nothing checked that the
# intended files actually got staged. Real protection against an actual
# accidentally-synced private key already exists in the sync workflow's own
# "Secret / personal-identifier safety gate" step, which scans file
# *content* for "-----BEGIN (RSA|PRIVATE|OPENSSH) PRIVATE KEY-----" --
# correctly catching real private keys regardless of extension, and
# correctly not flagging a public certificate that happens to be named
# *.pem, which the filename-based rule here couldn't distinguish.
